    <statement><p>Suppose <m>P(x,y)</m> is some binary predicate defined on a very small domain of discourse: just the integers 1, 2, 3, and 4. 
For each of the 16 pairs of these numbers, <m>P(x,y)</m> is either true or false, according to the following table (<m>x</m> values are rows, <m>y</m> values are columns).</p>
<tabular valign="top">
<col halign="right" right="minor"/>
<col halign="center"/>
<col halign="center"/>
<col halign="center"/>
<col halign="center"/>
<row bottom="minor" header="yes">
<cell> </cell>
<cell>1</cell>
<cell>2</cell>
<cell>3</cell>
<cell>4</cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell>1</cell>
<cell>T</cell>
<cell>F</cell>
<cell>F</cell>
<cell>F</cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell>2</cell>
<cell>F</cell>
<cell>T</cell>
<cell>T</cell>
<cell>F</cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell>3</cell>
<cell>T</cell>
<cell>T</cell>
<cell>T</cell>
<cell>T</cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell>4</cell>
<cell>F</cell>
<cell>F</cell>
<cell>F</cell>
<cell>F</cell>
</row>
</tabular>
<p>For example, <m>P(1,3)</m> is false, as indicated by the F in the first row, third column.</p>
<p>Use the table to decide whether the following statements are true or false.</p>
<p><ol>
<li>
<p><m>\exists y \forall x P(x,y)\text{.}</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>\forall y \exists x P(x,y)\text{.}</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>\exists x \forall y P(x,y)\text{.}</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>\forall x \exists y P(x,y)\text{.}</m></p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</statement>
